proofs.proof_tex:
Fix \(\ell\) and temporarily suppress that index.  Write \(n_j=n_{\ell j}\), \(r_j=\rho_nc_{\ell j,n}\), and \(a_n=\sqrt{\rho_n/n}\).  The add-half estimator has the exact decomposition
\[
\widetilde r_j-r_j
=\frac{K_j-n_jr_j}{n_j+1}+\frac{1/2-r_j}{n_j+1}.
\tag{1}
\]
The role-share lower bound makes the second term \(O(n^{-1})=o(a_n)\), because \(n\rho_n\to\infty\).  For fixed real \(t_1,\ldots,t_4\), independence of all role observations gives a product characteristic function for the centered sums in (1).  Expanding each logarithm through its quadratic term yields
\[
\log\mathbb E\exp\!\left\{i\sum_{j=1}^4t_j\sqrt{\frac n{\rho_n}}
\frac{K_j-n_jr_j}{n_j+1}\right\}
=-\frac12\sum_{j=1}^4t_j^2\frac{c_{\ell j}}{\pi_{\ell j}}+o(1).
\tag{2}
\]
Indeed, the variance of coordinate \(j\), after multiplication by \(n/\rho_n\), is
\[
\frac n{\rho_n}\frac{n_jr_j(1-r_j)}{(n_j+1)^2}
\longrightarrow\frac{c_{\ell j}}{\pi_{\ell j}},
\]
by the common-rarity, coefficient-limit, role-share-limit, and rare-limit assumptions.  The sum of the absolute third-order remainders is at most a fixed multiple of
\[
n_j\left\{\frac{\sqrt{n/\rho_n}}{n_j+1}\right\}^{\!3}r_j
=O\{(n\rho_n)^{-1/2}\}=o(1),
\]
uniformly for fixed \(t_j\).  Tightness follows from the displayed bounded second moments, and Fourier inversion first for smooth compactly supported test functions and then by approximation identifies the only possible limit from (2).  Thus
\[
a_n^{-1}(\widetilde r-r)
\Rightarrow
N\!\left(0,\operatorname{diag}\left(
\frac{c_{\ell1}}{\pi_{\ell1}},\ldots,
\frac{c_{\ell4}}{\pi_{\ell4}}
\right)\right).
\tag{3}
\]
The same calculation for the fixed collection of \(4L=60\) cells, together with disjoint-role independence, proves joint convergence and independence across pairs.

Let \(P(p)=p_1p_2/p_3-p_4\).  All true and estimated probabilities lie in \((0,1)\), so the denominator is positive.  At \(r=\rho_n(c_1,c_2,c_3,c_4)\),
\[
\nabla P(r)=
\left(\frac{c_2}{c_3},\frac{c_1}{c_3},
-\frac{c_1c_2}{c_3^2},-1\right).
\tag{4}
\]
By (1), Chebyshev's inequality, the coefficient box, and \(n\rho_n\to\infty\), the event
\[
\mathcal E_n=\left\{\max_{1\leq j\leq4}
|\widetilde r_j/r_j-1|\leq\frac12\right\}
\]
has probability tending to one.  On every line segment joining \(r\) to \(\widetilde r\) in \(\mathcal E_n\), the first three coordinates equal \(\rho_n d_j\) with \(d_j\in[1/4,6]\).  Direct differentiation shows that the Hessian of \(P\) is \(O(\rho_n^{-1})\), uniformly on this set.  Since (3) gives \(\|\widetilde r-r\|=O_P(a_n)\), Taylor's theorem gives
\[
P(\widetilde r)-P(r)
=\nabla P(r)^{\!\top}(\widetilde r-r)+O_P(n^{-1})
=\nabla P(r)^{\!\top}(\widetilde r-r)+o_P(a_n).
\tag{5}
\]
Equations (3)--(5), coefficient convergence, and the strict positivity of every \(c_{\ell j}\) and \(\pi_{\ell j}\) imply
\[
\frac{\widehat\Delta^P_\ell-\Delta^P_\ell}
{\sqrt{\rho_nV_\ell/n}}\Rightarrow N(0,1),
\tag{6}
\]
because the variance of the limiting linear form is
\[
\frac{c_{\ell4}}{\pi_{\ell4}}
+Q_\ell^2\sum_{j=1}^3\frac1{\pi_{\ell j}c_{\ell j}}
=V_\ell>0.
\tag{7}
\]

We now compare the exact-link map with \(P\) at the stochastic scale.  The common-cutoff calculation in the proof of the uniform theorem used only that the coefficient interval was a fixed compact subset of \((0,\infty)\).  Replacing \([1/2,4]\) there by \([1/4,6]\) therefore gives, uniformly on \(\mathcal E_n\),
\[
H(p_1,p_2,p_3)=\frac{p_1p_2}{p_3}\{1+O(u_n^{-2})\},
\qquad
\frac{m(q(p_1)+q(p_2)-q(p_3))}{m(q(p_j))}
=1+O(u_n^{-2}).
\tag{8}
\]
For \(s=q(p_1)+q(p_2)-q(p_3)\) and \((e_1,e_2,e_3)=(1,1,-1)\), direct differentiation, using \(q'(p)=1/\phi(q(p))\), gives
\[
H_j(p)=e_j\frac{\phi(s)}{\phi(q(p_j))},
\]
and
\[
H_{jk}(p)=H_j(p)\left\{
-\frac{s e_k}{\phi(q(p_k))}
+\mathbf1\{j=k\}\frac{q(p_j)}{\phi(q(p_j))}
\right\}.
\tag{9}
\]
Since \(\phi(v)=\Phi(v)m(v)\), (8)--(9) imply
\[
\nabla\{H(p_1,p_2,p_3)-p_4\}\big|_{p=r}
=\nabla P(r)+O(u_n^{-2}).
\tag{10}
\]
The common-cutoff bounds also give \(|s|+|q(p_j)|=O(u_n)\) and \(\phi(q(p_j))\asymp\rho_nu_n\) on \(\mathcal E_n\).  Hence (9) bounds the exact-map Hessian by \(O(\rho_n^{-1})\).  Taylor's theorem, (3), and (10) consequently yield
\[
\{\widehat\Delta^E_\ell-\Delta_\ell\}
-\{\widehat\Delta^P_\ell-\Delta^P_\ell\}
=o_P(a_n).
\tag{11}
\]
Here \(\Delta_\ell=H(r_{\ell1},r_{\ell2},r_{\ell3})-r_{\ell4}\) follows from the published-identification proposition inherited through the exact-factorization and common-cutoff dependencies; it is the causal target, whereas the two hatted quantities are observed-count functionals.  Combining (6), (7), and (11) proves
\[
\frac{\widehat\Delta^E_\ell-\Delta_\ell}
{\sqrt{\rho_nV_\ell/n}}\Rightarrow N(0,1).
\tag{12}
\]

The exact factorization and the uniform common-cutoff theorem give, with no division by \(A_{\ell n}\),
\[
\frac{\Delta^P_{\ell n}-\Delta_{\ell n}}
{\sqrt{\rho_nV_\ell/n}}
=\frac{Q_{\ell n}}{\sqrt{V_\ell}}
\frac{A_{\ell n}\sqrt{n\rho_n}}{u_n^2}
\{1+O(u_n^{-2})\}
=\frac{Q_\ell}{\sqrt{V_\ell}}\Lambda_{\ell n}+o(1)
\tag{13}
\]
whenever \(\Lambda_{\ell n}\to\lambda_\ell\).  The sign in (13) follows from \(\Delta-\Delta^P=\rho QA C\) and \(C=-u^{-2}\{1+O(u^{-2})\}\).  Adding (13) to (6) proves the asserted noncentral normal limit.  Because there are only \(L=15\) pairs, the joint characteristic-function calculation following (3) proves all these limits jointly with independent coordinates.

Finally, (11) and (13), without assuming convergence of \(\Lambda_{\ell n}\), give
\[
\frac{\widehat\Delta^P_\ell-\widehat\Delta^E_\ell}
{\sqrt{\rho_nV_\ell/n}}
=\frac{Q_{\ell n}}{\sqrt{V_\ell}}\Lambda_{\ell n}
\{1+O(u_n^{-2})\}+o_P(1).
\tag{14}
\]
Coefficient and share limits make \(Q_{\ell n}/\sqrt{V_\ell}\) converge to a finite strictly positive number.  Thus the left side of (14) converges to zero in probability if \(\Lambda_{\ell n}\to0\).  Conversely, if the left side converges to zero in probability, its deterministic leading term must converge to zero, for otherwise a subsequence bounded away from zero contradicts the \(o_P(1)\) remainder; hence \(\Lambda_{\ell n}\to0\).  This proves the stated if-and-only-if first-order equivalence, including both exact cancellation planes.
